<p id="S1.T1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.T1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a><span id="S1.T1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">]</span></cite><span id="S1.T1.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> also gives a zero-freeness result on a part of a circle, which is not a region.</span></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Zero-free regions for 2-spin systems<span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_centering ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:111%;">2</span></span>
            
            
            
          </span></span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For each zero-free region in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, an FPTAS exists via Barvinok’s algorithm.
In contrast, Weitz-style FPTASes are currently known only for the regions in Rows 1, 2, 4, 6, and 7, where SSM can be established via tree recurrence arguments using contraction properties.
For other regions, particularly those corresponding to the ferromagnetic regime (<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>), it remains unclear whether SSM can be proved using such methods.
This raises a natural and intriguing question: <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">For those zero-free regions of 2-spin systems where FPTASes exist via Barvinok’s algorithm, can Weitz’s algorithm also be applied? In particular, is it possible to establish SSM for these zero-free regions even when the standard tree recurrence method is not known to work?</em></p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">New SSM results and Weitz’s FPTASes beyond tree recurrences</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we answer this question in the affirmative.
We show that for any zero-free region of 2-spin systems on graphs with pinned vertices, SSM holds throughout the same region.
This immediately implies new SSM results including the regions in Rows 3 and 5 of Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
We also obtain new SSM results for parts of the Lee-Yang zero-free region.
Consequently, Weitz’s FPTASes exist for these regions.
Notably, these SSM results are <em id="S1.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> known to be provable via the standard tree recurrence method.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The SSM property holds for every zero-free region in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
In addition, for the ferromagnetic Ising model (<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>), the SSM property holds for the regions
<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\lambda\in\mathbb{C}\mid|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda\in\mathbb{C}\mid|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\lambda\in\mathbb{C}\mid|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda\in\mathbb{C}\mid|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a consequence, Weitz’s FPTASes exist for these regions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The conditions <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to subsets of the zero-free regions <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> established by the Lee-Yang circle theorem.
At first glance, establishing SSM under these conditions might appear feasible via a standard tree recurrence argument.
However, to the best of our knowledge, <em id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">no such tree recurrence proof is known, or even believed feasible</em>.
The tree recurrence method typically relies on carefully designed potential functions, the construction and analysis of which can be highly challenging (e.g., see Section 4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>).
For instance, in antiferromagnetic 2-spin systems, such a function has been constructed only within the uniqueness region, exhibiting a complicated form expressed as an antiderivative of <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\sqrt{x(\beta x+1)(x+\gamma)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\sqrt{x(\beta x+1)(x+\gamma)}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given the difficulty in constructing potential functions, whether one can be constructed for ferromagnetic 2-spin systems remains an open question. We overcome this obstacle by presenting a direct proof that establishes <em id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">novel</em> SSM results without employing the tree recurrence method.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, we introduce two variants of spatial mixing, namely <em id="Thmremark1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">plus spatial mixing</em> (PSM) and <em id="Thmremark1.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">minus spatial mixing</em> (MSM) (see Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem40" title="Definition 40 (Plus or minus spatial mixing (PSM/MSM)). ‣ 5 The implication for non-uniform external fields ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>)
corresponding to Ising models with all-plus and all-minus boundary conditions, respectively.
We establish them for the ferromagnetic Ising model in broader parameter regimes <math id="Thmremark1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&lt;1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&lt;1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Related work</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work generalizes a recent line of work establishing that zero-freeness implies SSM for the hard-core model and other graph homomorphism models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.
This implication reflects a deeper connection between the two key paradigms for designing FPTASes for 2-spin systems: zero-freeness (from complex analysis) and correlation decay (from probabilistic arguments).
In seminal works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, Dobrushin and Shlosman proved that for lattice models, the correlation decay property is equivalent to the zero-freeness of the partition function.
Recently, further formal connections have been established beyond lattice models.
Such results have been obtained for the hard-core model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, the Ising model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and general 2-spin systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> on graphs with bounded degree.
In these works, for real parameters where correlation decay can be established via the tree recurrence method using the contraction property, this property can often be analytically extended to complex neighborhoods of the parameters, thereby ensuring zero-freeness of the partition function.
However, it remains unclear whether correlation decay or SSM directly implies zero-freeness of the partition function.
On the other hand, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> proved correlation decay result for hard-core model based on multivariate zero-freeness condition on polydisc region, establishing Weitz’s type FPTAS.
Gamarnik <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> first showed that zero-freeness of the partition function
directly implies a weak form of SSM for the hard-core model and other
graph homomorphism models.
Later, Regts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> strengthened this result by proving that zero-freeness implies SSM for the hard-core model near <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as for other graph homomorphism models on all graphs of bounded degree.
When restricted to 2-spin systems, the graph homomorphism model is
the 2-spin system without external field, i.e., <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the implication from zero-freeness to SSM holds for a complex neighborhood of <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma)=(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma)=(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we significantly extend the known implication from zero-freeness to the most general settings of 2-spin systems, comprehensively covering all currently known zero-free regions (Rows 1 to 7 of Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, to the best of our knowledge) of the partition functions of 2-spin systems with pinned vertices.
The prior approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> relies on an analytic tool, namely cluster expansions, which are model-specific and known only for a few cases such as the hard-core model (i.e., <math id="Sx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0,\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0,\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>) near <math id="Sx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>, or the 2-spin system without external field (i.e., <math id="Sx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>) near <math id="Sx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
A crucial limitation of this approach is that
beyond these model-specific settings, general cluster expansions are not known for 2-spin systems with arbitrary complex parameters <math id="Sx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> on general graphs.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
          While there are cluster expansion results for lattice structures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, it is unknown whether these results can be extended to general graphs.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We circumvent this obstacle and establish SSM for general 2-spin systems via a purely combinatorial approach based on a <em id="Sx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Christoffel–Darboux-type identity</em> (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> below), which holds universally for arbitrary complex <math id="Sx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. This enables a unified analysis of various zero-free regions across all three parameters.
Unlike the previous result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, which treats the partition function as a univariate function restricted to strip-like zero-free regions,
we are able to handle the partition function <math id="Sx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> with arbitrary <em id="Sx1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multivariate</em> complex parameters <math id="Sx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and zero-free regions of arbitrary shape.
In summary, we present a unified and broadly applicable framework for deriving SSM from zero-freeness based on a combinatorial identity, bypassing the need for model-specific cluster expansions which may be inapplicable to general 2-spin systems.
In Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Comparison with the cluster expansion approach ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, we review the cluster expansion approach in more detail, discuss its limitations in extending to general 2-spin systems, and compare it with our approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we formally describe the implication from zero-freeness to SSM for general 2-spin systems.
A region refers to a standard complex region, i.e., a simply connected open set in <math id="Sx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a neighborhood of some <math id="Sx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to a region containing <math id="Sx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="Sx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say <math id="Sx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-neighborhood of <math id="Sx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> if either <math id="Sx1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a neighborhood of <math id="Sx1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Sx1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\{z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\{z\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a region <math id="Sx1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Sx1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="Sx1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U</annotation></semantics></math> to be the set <math id="Sx1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z\in\mathbb{C}\mid 1/z\in U\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z\in\mathbb{C}\mid 1/z\in U\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is a region.
A family <math id="Sx1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of graphs is closed under constructions of <em id="Sx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-avoiding walk trees</em> (see Subsection <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Weitz’s algorithm and self-avoiding walk tree ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) if for any graph <math id="Sx1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and any vertex <math id="Sx1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Sx1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the SAW tree of <math id="Sx1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> rooted at <math id="Sx1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="Sx1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs closed under self-avoiding walk </span>(SAW)<span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> tree construction, and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2},U_{3}\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2},U_{3}\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be one of the following cases:</span></p>
<ol id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are quasi-neighborhoods of some </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> respectively where </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are not both zero, and
</span><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a neighborhood of </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:-2.2pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are quasi-neighborhoods of some </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> respectively where </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a region containing </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:-2.2pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are quasi-neighborhoods of some </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> respectively where </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a region containing </span><math id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Denote <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((U_{1}\times U_{2})\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((U_{1}\times U_{2})\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose for any graph <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> (Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 5 (Feasible partial configuration). ‣ 2.1 Notations and definitions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the 2-spin system defined on <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits SSM (Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem7" title="Definition 7 (Strong spatial mixing, SSM). ‣ 2.1 Notations and definitions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, when <math id="Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is
a family of graphs of bounded degree, the parameters <math id="Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be relaxed to any nonzero complex numbers satisfying <math id="Thmtheorem2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}\gamma_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}\gamma_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Roughly speaking, the first condition of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> requires that the partition function viewed as a function of <math id="Sx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free in a complex neighborhood of <math id="Sx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second and third conditions of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> together require that the partition function viewed as a function of <math id="Sx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Sx1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free in a neighborhood of <math id="Sx1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to illustrate Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The first row and the second row are examples of conditions 1 and 2 respectively, where the underlying graphs are arbitrary and a non-negative point <math id="Sx1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is required. The third row illustrates condition 3, where the underlying graphs are required to have bounded degree, but no non-negative point is required.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2401.09317v4/condition_region.png" id="Sx1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:545/386;" width="545" height="386" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Examples of regions for the 3 conditions in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, for the Ising model, we have the following SSM result from zero-free regions of <math id="Sx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs closed under </span>SAW<span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> tree constructions, <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonzero complex number, and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that for any graph <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\lambda_{0}}(\beta)=Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\beta,\lambda_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\lambda_{0}}(\beta)=Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\beta,\lambda_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding Ising model specified by <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Although we give three different conditions involving three parameters <math id="Sx1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda,\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Sx1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> for 2-spin systems (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and a condition for Ising models (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) under which zero-freeness implies SSM, they are all proved by a unifying approach in which a Christoffel-Darboux type identity plays a key role.
A Christoffel-Darboux type identity was originally established for the independence polynomial on trees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and was later extended to general graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
Our main technical contribution lies in
establishing a Christoffel-Darboux type identity for the 2-spin system<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
            
            
            
          In fact, we establish an identity for the more general <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-spin system in the appendix, see Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem42" title="Theorem 42. ‣ Appendix A Christoffel-Darboux identity for 𝑞-spin systems on trees ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.</span></span></span> on trees.
Such an identity provides a relation for the partition functions of 2-spin systems with different pinned vertices, and may be of independent interest.
Given a graph <math id="Sx1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, a partial configuration <math id="Sx1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Sx1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in V(G)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in V(G),</annotation></semantics></math> we define</p>
<table id="Sx1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

