</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">With the lemma in hand, in order to obtain SSM, it suffices to establish the following two properties, namely a uniform bound on a circle and
local dependence of coefficients (LDC).
The first property says that there exists a circle <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the rational functions <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> have a uniform bound on <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> for any graph <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>,
any feasible partial configuration <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and any <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to it.
The second property states that if partial configurations <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> share the same local structure around a vertex <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> which is proper to both configurations, then the leading Taylor expansion coefficients of the two rational functions <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}_{G,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}_{G,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}_{G,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}_{G,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> coincide.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>,
a uniform bound was obtained by applying Montel’s theorem (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Montel’s Theorem). ‣ 2.3 Tools from complex analysis ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
In this paper, we use the same idea and extend it to the multivariate version since
we need to handle three complex parameters <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, LDC was proved by using the cluster expansion formula for polymer models, since explicit expressions were given for the Taylor series of the rational function <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0,\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0,\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the rational function <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> (after a variable replacement) where <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma,\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma,\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, besides these particular parameters, to the best of our knowledge, explicit cluster expansions are not known for 2-spin systems on general graphs with arbitrary complex parameters <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this paper, we overcome this obstacle and
prove LDC for general 2-spin systems with arbitrary <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> by establishing a Christoffel-Darboux type identity.
It turns out that an explicit expression for the Taylor series of the rational function is unnecessary for proving LDC for 2-spin systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Christoffel-Darboux type identity gives LDC</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove our main technical result, a Christoffel-Darboux type identity for 2-spin systems on trees and use it to prove local dependence of coefficients (LDC) for <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda,\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in a unified way.
We first formally define LDC.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 15</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Local dependence of coefficients (LDC) for <math id="Thmtheorem15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> near 0)<span id="Thmtheorem15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
