<p id="S3.p12.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p12.4.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational function on <math id="S3.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> which is analytic near <math id="S3.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the coefficients of the term <math id="S3.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq k\leq d_{G}(u,v)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq k\leq d_{G}(u,v)-1</annotation></semantics></math> in the Taylor series of them are the same.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The above proof of LDC for the hard-core model using the Christoffel-Darboux type identity may somewhat answer the following interesting question: <em id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">if the partition function of the hard-core model is zero-free on a complex neighborhood of some point <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, can we get SSM for this zero-free region?</em>
The answer would be yes if the LDC property could hold for rational functions <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}^{v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}^{v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> expanded as Taylor series near <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, the Christoffel-Darboux type identity implies that such a LDC property does not hold. Otherwise, for any graph <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and any vertex <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, we would have <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}(\lambda)-P^{u^{+}}_{G,v}(\lambda)=(\lambda-\lambda_{0})^{d(u,v)}H(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}(\lambda)-P^{u^{+}}_{G,v}(\lambda)=(\lambda-\lambda_{0})^{d(u,v)}H(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> for some function <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> analytic near <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One can easily check that this is false.
Thus, it is highly possible that a zero-free region not containing <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> cannot imply SSM (at least not by our approach).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we extend the above Christoffel-Darboux type identity to general 2-spin systems on trees. Actually, we can extend it to the more general <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-spins system on trees (see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Christoffel-Darboux identity for 𝑞-spin systems on trees ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 22</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> restated)<span id="Thmtheorem22.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree, <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a partial configuration on some <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> which may be empty, and <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are two distinct vertices in <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\backslash\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\backslash\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the unique path in <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(p_{uv})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(p_{uv})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of points in <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[p_{uv}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[p_{uv}]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of neighbors of points in <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[p_{uv}]\setminus V(p_{uv})=\{v_{1},\cdots,v_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[p_{uv}]\setminus V(p_{uv})=\{v_{1},\cdots,v_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the component of <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\setminus p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\setminus p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then for the partition function of the 2-spin system defined on <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> conditioning on <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have (we omit the argument <math id="Thmtheorem22.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> in the second line for simplicity)</span></p>
