<div id="S3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Lemmas <a href="#Thmtheorem18" title="Lemma 18. ‣ 3 Christoffel-Darboux type identity gives LDC ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>, <a href="#Thmtheorem19" title="Lemma 19. ‣ 3 Christoffel-Darboux type identity gives LDC ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem23" title="Lemma 23. ‣ 3 Christoffel-Darboux type identity gives LDC ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>, we have the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 24</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem24.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix complex parameters <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, a complex neighborhood <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, and a family of graphs <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> closed under </span>SAW<span id="Thmtheorem24.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> tree constructions. If the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>
is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\backslash\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\backslash\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the corresponding 2-spin system satisfies LDC on <math id="Thmtheorem24.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that in the Christoffel-Darboux type identity for the 2-spin system, besides the “factor” <math id="S3.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{d(u,v)+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{d(u,v)+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can also take a “factor” <math id="S3.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta\gamma-1)^{d(u,v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta\gamma-1)^{d(u,v)}</annotation></semantics></math> out of <math id="S3.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{T,u,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+,+}Z_{T,u,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},-,-}-Z_{T,u,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+,-}Z_{T,u,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},-,+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{T,u,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+,+}Z_{T,u,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},-,-}-Z_{T,u,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+,-}Z_{T,u,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},-,+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we can prove LDC for <math id="S3.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> similarly.
The proof of LDC for <math id="S3.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 25</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem25.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix complex parameters <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of graphs closed under </span>SAW<span id="Thmtheorem25.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> tree constructions and <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> such that the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\gamma,\lambda}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\gamma,\lambda}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the corresponding 2-spin system satisfies LDC for <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="Thmtheorem25.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">It suffices to prove that the system satisfies point-to-point LDC for <math id="S3.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the SAW tree construction and an analogue of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem19" title="Lemma 19. ‣ 3 Christoffel-Darboux type identity gives LDC ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> for <math id="S3.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, we only need to consider the case that <math id="S3.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree.
Then by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem22" title="Theorem 22 (Theorem restated). ‣ 3 Christoffel-Darboux type identity gives LDC ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex27X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex27X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{+}}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{+}}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
