<p id="S3.p22.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p22.2.1" class="ltx_text">for some function <math id="S3.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> analytic near <math id="S3.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
The same form of equation holds for <math id="S3.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{-}}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{-}}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We are done with the proof of LDC for <math id="S3.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p23.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, for the Ising model, <math id="S3.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S3.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, similarly, we can define and prove LDC for <math id="S3.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> for the Ising model.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 26</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(LDC for <math id="Thmtheorem26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="Thmtheorem26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmtheorem26.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem26.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem26.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a complex parameter <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, a neighborhood <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (or respectively <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>), and a family of graphs <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding Ising model (defined on <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> specified with the fixed parameter <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and a variable <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in U</annotation></semantics></math>) is said to satisfy LDC for <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (or respectively <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>) if for
any <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>,
any feasible configurations <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\in\{0,1\}^{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\in\{0,1\}^{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}\in\{0,1\}^{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}\in\{0,1\}^{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and any vertex <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the functions <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> are analytic on <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, and their corresponding Taylor series <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum^{\infty}_{i=0}a_{i}(\beta-1)^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum^{\infty}_{i=0}a_{i}(\beta-1)^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum^{\infty}_{i=0}b_{i}(\beta-1)^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum^{\infty}_{i=0}b_{i}(\beta-1)^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> (or respectively <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum^{\infty}_{i=0}a_{i}(\beta+1)^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum^{\infty}_{i=0}a_{i}(\beta+1)^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum^{\infty}_{i=0}b_{i}(\beta+1)^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum^{\infty}_{i=0}b_{i}(\beta+1)^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=-1</annotation></semantics></math>) satisfy <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmtheorem26.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq d_{G}(v,\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}})-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq d_{G}(v,\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}})-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 27</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of graphs closed under </span>SAW<span id="Thmtheorem27.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> tree constructions and <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (or respectively <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>) such that <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\gamma,\lambda}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\gamma,\lambda}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the corresponding Ising model satisfies LDC for <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (or respectively <math id="Thmtheorem27.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Zero-freeness implies SSM</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now, we are ready to show that zero-freeness implies SSM.
We first apply Montel’s theorem (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Montel’s Theorem). ‣ 2.3 Tools from complex analysis ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) to get a uniform bound for the multivariate function <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, which extends Lemma 5 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 28</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem28.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, and <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2},U_{3}\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2},U_{3}\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be quasi-neighborhoods of some <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}\times U_{2}\times U_{3}\neq\{(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}\times U_{2}\times U_{3}\neq\{(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})\}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., at least one of <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex region).
Denote the set <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((U_{1}\times U_{2})\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((U_{1}\times U_{2})\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function of the 2-spin system defined on <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, and one of the following conditions holds.</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are non-negative real numbers that are not all zero;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is
a family of graphs of bounded degree and </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}\gamma_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}\gamma_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmtheorem28.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem28.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then for any compact set <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for every <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, any vertex <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)|\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)|\leq M</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem28.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We give the proof when <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are neighborhoods of some points.
For the case that some of them are singletons, the proof is identical using Montel’s theorem (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Montel’s Theorem). ‣ 2.3 Tools from complex analysis ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) with fewer variables.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose to the contrary that there exists a sequence of tuples <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G_{n}\in\mathcal{G},v_{n},\Lambda_{n},\sigma^{(n)}_{\Lambda_{n}},(\beta_{n},\gamma_{n},\lambda_{n})\in S)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G_{n}\in\mathcal{G},v_{n},\Lambda_{n},\sigma^{(n)}_{\Lambda_{n}},(\beta_{n},\gamma_{n},\lambda_{n})\in S)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|P_{G_{n},v_{n}}^{\sigma^{(n)}_{\Lambda_{n}}}(\beta_{n},\gamma_{n},\lambda_{n})\right|\geq n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|P_{G_{n},v_{n}}^{\sigma^{(n)}_{\Lambda_{n}}}(\beta_{n},\gamma_{n},\lambda_{n})\right|\geq n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">We denote by <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{n}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{n}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> the sequence of functions <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P_{G_{n},v_{n}}^{\sigma^{(n)}_{\Lambda_{n}}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{G_{n},v_{n}}^{\sigma^{(n)}_{\Lambda_{n}}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the partition function of the 2-spin system defined on <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, any feasible partial configuration <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> on some <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math>, and any vertex <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the function <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic on <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bf U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bf U</annotation></semantics></math> (which is connected since <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}\times U_{2}\setminus\{(0,0)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}\times U_{2}\setminus\{(0,0)\}</annotation></semantics></math> are connected) and avoids the values <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus all such functions form a normal family by Montel’s theorem (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Montel’s Theorem). ‣ 2.3 Tools from complex analysis ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we consider the two conditions. If <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and they are not all zero, then for any rational function <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}</annotation></semantics></math>, its value given the input <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability which lies in the interval <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Otherwise, <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of graphs the degrees of which are bounded by some <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}\gamma_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}\gamma_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, one can check that for any rational function <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}</annotation></semantics></math>, its value given the input <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\lambda_{0}\beta_{0}^{d_{v}}}{\lambda_{0}\beta_{0}^{d_{v}}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\lambda_{0}\beta_{0}^{d_{v}}}{\lambda_{0}\beta_{0}^{d_{v}}+1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is the degree of <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})\neq\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})\neq\infty</annotation></semantics></math> since the partition function is nonzero on <math id="S4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by the maximum degree <math id="S4.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>,
for all rational functions <math id="S4.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have <math id="S4.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})|\leq\max_{0\leq d_{v}\leq\Delta}\left|\frac{\lambda_{0}\beta_{0}^{d_{v}}}{\lambda_{0}\beta_{0}^{d_{v}}+1}\right|&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta_{0},\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0})|\leq\max_{0\leq d_{v}\leq\Delta}\left|\frac{\lambda_{0}\beta_{0}^{d_{v}}}{\lambda_{0}\beta_{0}^{d_{v}}+1}\right|&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, in both cases, any sequence in the family of rational functions <math id="S4.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}</annotation></semantics></math> can not converge locally uniformly to <math id="S4.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Definition 11 (Normal family). ‣ 2.3 Tools from complex analysis ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, every sequence has a sub-sequence converging locally uniformly to a holomorphic function.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, the above sequence <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{n}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{n}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a sub-sequence <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{n_{i}}\}_{i\in\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{n_{i}}\}_{i\in\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> converging locally uniformly to some holomorphic function <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact subset of <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the convergence is uniform on <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and the function <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by some <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, there exists some <math id="S4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}\geq n_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}\geq n_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> , we have <math id="S4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f_{n_{i}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)|&lt;2C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f_{n_{i}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)|&lt;2C</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Clearly, this contradicts that for all <math id="S4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f_{n}(\beta_{n},\gamma_{n},\lambda_{n})|\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f_{n}(\beta_{n},\gamma_{n},\lambda_{n})|\geq n</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta_{n},\gamma_{n},\lambda_{n})\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta_{n},\gamma_{n},\lambda_{n})\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6</span></span><span id="Thmremark6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, when the set <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem28" title="Lemma 28. ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a> is a complex neighborhood of <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, we may take the compact set <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{1}\}\times\{\gamma_{1}\}\times\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{1}\}\times\{\gamma_{1}\}\times\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> for some arbitrary <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}\in U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}\in U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}\in U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}\in U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}\subseteq U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}\subseteq U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we get a uniform bound for <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v,\beta_{1},\gamma_{1}}(\lambda)=P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta_{1},\gamma_{1},\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v,\beta_{1},\gamma_{1}}(\lambda)=P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta_{1},\gamma_{1},\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> on the circle <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly,
by taking <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\gamma_{1})\times\{\gamma_{1}\}\times\{\lambda_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\gamma_{1})\times\{\gamma_{1}\}\times\{\lambda_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
one can get a uniform bound for <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v,_{\gamma_{1},\lambda_{1}}}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v,_{\gamma_{1},\lambda_{1}}}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> on the circle <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\gamma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\gamma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and by taking <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{1}\}\times\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\beta_{1})\times\{\lambda_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{1}\}\times\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\beta_{1})\times\{\lambda_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
one can get a uniform bound for <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v,_{\beta_{1},\lambda_{1}}}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v,_{\beta_{1},\lambda_{1}}}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> on the circle <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\beta_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\beta_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Then, combining LDC and the above uniform bound, we can prove SSM on a disk region.
Still, we give the proof of SSM for <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> as an illustration.
The proof of SSM for <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is similar.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 29</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem29.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs closed under </span>SAW<span id="Thmtheorem29.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> tree constructions, <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be quasi-neighborhoods of some <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and
<math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((U_{1}\times U_{2})\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((U_{1}\times U_{2})\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function of the 2-spin systems defined on <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, and one of the two conditions of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem28" title="Lemma 28. ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a> holds.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathbb{D}_{\rho}\subseteq U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathbb{D}_{\rho}\subseteq U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> for some disk <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding 2-spin system exhibits SSM with rate <math id="Thmtheorem29.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\rho/|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\rho/|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem29.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem29.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem29.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, when <math id="Thmtheorem29.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> itself is a disk <math id="Thmtheorem29.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="Thmtheorem29.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem29.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathbb{D}_{\rho}=U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathbb{D}_{\rho}=U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, for any <math id="Thmtheorem29.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the 2-spin system exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any fixed <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the 2-spin system defined on <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with fixed <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and a variable <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}\backslash\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}\backslash\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, it satisfies LDC by Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem24" title="Lemma 24. ‣ 3 Christoffel-Darboux type identity gives LDC ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>, and for any circle <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}\subseteq U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}\subseteq U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> it has a uniform bound on <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> by Remark <a href="#Thmremark6" title="Remark 6. ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
For some arbitrary <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>,
some feasible partial configurations <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and some vertex <math id="S4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to <math id="S4.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the rational functions <math id="S4.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\lambda)=P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\lambda)=P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(\lambda)=P_{G,v}^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(\lambda)=P_{G,v}^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. By LDC, these two functions are analytic and the coefficients of the first <math id="S4.p8.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{G}(v,\{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(v,\{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}\})</annotation></semantics></math> terms in their Taylor series are equal.
Also, by a uniform bound on the circle <math id="S4.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial{\mathbb{D}_{\rho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial{\mathbb{D}_{\rho}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S4.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S4.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P(\lambda)|\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P(\lambda)|\leq M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|Q(\lambda)|\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|Q(\lambda)|\leq M</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then the result follows directly from Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem14" title="Lemma 14. ‣ 2.3 Tools from complex analysis ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem29" title="Lemma 29. ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>, when the zero-free region of <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a disk, the 2-spin system exhibits SSM for any <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in the disk.
Using Riemann mapping theorem, we are able to map any region <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to the unit disk <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as a fixed point.
Finally, we can drop the restriction of <math id="S4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathbb{D}_{\rho}\subseteq U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathbb{D}_{\rho}\subseteq U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> for some disk <math id="S4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> as in the statement of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem29" title="Lemma 29. ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>, and get the following result: zero-freeness implies SSM for any complex neighborhood of <math id="S4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 30</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem30.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs closed under </span>SAW<span id="Thmtheorem30.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> tree constructions,
<math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be quasi-neighborhoods of some <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and
<math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((U_{1}\times U_{2})\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((U_{1}\times U_{2})\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function of the 2-spin systems defined on <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, and one of the two conditions of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem28" title="Lemma 28. ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a> holds.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem30.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\bf U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\bf U</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding 2-spin system exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Riemann mapping theorem (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 13 (Riemann mapping theorem). ‣ 2.3 Tools from complex analysis ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), there exists a conformal map <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{D}_{1}\to U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{D}_{1}\to U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">For a rational function <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is proper to the feasible partial configuration <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we consider the composition <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,f(\lambda))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,f(\lambda))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Clearly, <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic on <math id="S4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf U^{\prime}}=(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(\mathbb{D}_{1}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf U^{\prime}}=(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(\mathbb{D}_{1}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the partition function of the 2-spin system is zero-free <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>,
for any <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, any feasible partial configuration <math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and any vertex <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the functions <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> avoid the values <math id="S4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Still by Montel’s theorem (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 12 (Montel’s Theorem). ‣ 2.3 Tools from complex analysis ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), these functions form a normal family.
Then,
similar to the proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem28" title="Lemma 28. ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>,
we deduce that for any <math id="S4.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{1}\}\times\{\gamma_{1}\}\times\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}\subseteq{\bf U^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{1}\}\times\{\gamma_{1}\}\times\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}\subseteq{\bf U^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can get a uniform bound for <math id="S4.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v,\beta_{1},\gamma_{1}}(\lambda)=g^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta_{1},\gamma_{1},\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v,\beta_{1},\gamma_{1}}(\lambda)=g^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta_{1},\gamma_{1},\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> on the circle <math id="S4.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\lambda)=\lambda h(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\lambda)=\lambda h(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> analytic near <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also, for any fixed <math id="S4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
same as the proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem24" title="Lemma 24. ‣ 3 Christoffel-Darboux type identity gives LDC ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>, we have</span></p>
