<p id="A1.p4.5" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is already pinned to some <math id="A1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[q]</annotation></semantics></math>, then automatically <math id="A1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},i}_{G,v}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},i}_{G,v}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},j}_{G,v}=Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},j}_{G,v}=Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Given a partial configuration <math id="A1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="A1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\prime}\subset\Lambda," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\prime}\subset\Lambda,</annotation></semantics></math> we denote <math id="A1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> the restriction of <math id="A1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, called a sub-partial configuration of <math id="A1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the rest of this paper, we omit the argument <math id="A1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of the partition functions for simplicity.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now we generalize Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ A novel and unified approach beyond cluster expansions ‣ Strong Spatial Mixing for General 2-Spin Systems:A Unified Approach from Zero-Freeness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-spin systems as the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem42" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem42.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 42</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem42.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem42.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem42.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem42.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree, <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a partial configuration on some <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\neq v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\neq v</annotation></semantics></math> are two vertices in <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\backslash\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\backslash\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> denote the unique path in <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[P]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of neighbors of points in <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[P]\setminus P=\{v_{1},\cdots,v_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[P]\setminus P=\{v_{1},\cdots,v_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the component of <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\setminus P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\setminus P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then for the partition function of the <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-spin system defined on <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> conditioning on <math id="Thmtheorem42.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
