<p id="A1.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">it can be viewed as the determinant of the product of matrices <math id="A1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{T_{0},v_{0},u}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},k,t})_{1\leq k,t\leq q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{T_{0},v_{0},u}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},k,t})_{1\leq k,t\leq q}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus it equals to the product of their determinants. Now we have</span></p>
